  1. Unintended Permanent Deformation (A Bug)
  
  If your object is supposed to be elastic (like a bouncy ball or rigid crate) but gets permanently squished or inverted after an impact, it is usually caused by one of three issues:
  • Lack of cross-bracing (Shear/Bending constraints). A simple grid or polygon made only of outer edge springs has no structural rigidity. If a square is crushed flat, edge lengths remain valid even though the shape has collapsed.
  • Mesh inversion (Flipping). Under extreme force, a vertex can get pushed completely across an opposite edge. Once inverted, distance constraints happily hold the vertex in its new, inside-out position forever.
  • Insufficient constraint iterations. Verlet relies on iterative relaxation (Jakobsen method) to satisfy distance constraints. If the iteration count per frame is too low, springs cannot fully resolve large displacements before the next frame updates old_position, baking the squished state into the object's momentum.
  
  How to Fix It
  
  • Add internal structural constraints. Connect non-adjacent vertices with diagonal cross-springs (for quads/grids) or all-to-all springs (for small convex polygons) to lock internal angles.
  • Increase solver iterations. Run your constraint satisfaction loop 5 to 15 times per physics step instead of once.
  • Use Shape Matching. Keep a copy of the object's original rest shape. Each frame, calculate the best-fit rotation and translation for the current vertices, and pull each vertex slightly toward its ideal rest position.
  • Add area/volume preservation. Compute the signed area of your polygon each frame; if it drops below the rest area (or becomes negative due to inversion), push vertices outward along their normals.
